{
  "generated_text": "To determine the coordinates of point \\(D\\) in parallelogram \\(ABCD\\), we can use the property that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. This means that the midpoint of diagonal \\(AC\\) is the same as the midpoint of diagonal \\(BD\\).\n\nFirst, we find the midpoint of diagonal \\(AC\\). The coordinates of \\(A\\) are \\((0, 1)\\) and the coordinates of \\(C\\) are \\((4, 0)\\). The formula for the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{0 + 4}{2}, \\frac{1 + 0}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{4}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThis midpoint \\(\\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\\) is also the midpoint of diagonal \\(BD\\). Let the coordinates of \\(D\\) be \\((x, y)\\). The coordinates of \\(B\\) are \\((2, 1)\\). Using the midpoint formula again for diagonal \\(BD\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{2 + x}{2}, \\frac{1 + y}{2} \\right) = \\left( 2, \\frac{1}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two equations:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 + x}{2} = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1 + y}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 + x}{2} = 2 \\implies 2 + x = 4 \\implies x = 2\n\\]\n\nSolving the second equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + y}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies 1 + y = 1 \\implies y = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the coordinates of \\(D\\) are \\((2, 0)\\). The sum of the coordinates of point \\(D\\) is:\n\n\\[\n2 + 0 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
